Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:
```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = [];
  let currentAdjective;

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i] === "beautiful" || words[i] === "green") {
      currentAdjective = words[i];
    } else if (currentAdjective !== undefined) {
      adjectives.push(currentAdjective);
      currentAdjective = undefined;
    }
  }

  if (currentAdjective !== undefined) {
    adjectives.push(currentAdjective);
  }

  const reversedAdjectives = adjectives.reverse();
  const newSentence = reversedAdjectives.join(" ") + " " + words.filter(word => !["beautiful", "green"].includes(word)).join(" ");

  return newSentence;
}
```
Here's an example of how you can use this function:
```javascript
const sentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const newSentence = switchAdjectives(sentence);
console.log(newSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```
This function splits the input sentence into individual words, identifies the adjectives ("beautiful" and "green" in this case), and then reverses their order. Finally, it concatenates the reversed adjectives with the rest of the sentence (excluding the adjectives) to create the output sentence.

