Here's a MATLAB code that can help identify the tense used in a given sentence:

```matlab
function tense = identify_tense(sentence)
    % Split the sentence into words
    words = strsplit(sentence, ' ');

    % Initialize variables to keep track of tense and verb
    current_tense = '';
    verb = '';

    % Loop through each word in the sentence
    for i = 1:length(words)
        % Check if the word is a verb
        if ismember(words{i}, {'is', 'are', 'was', 'were', 'be', 'being', 'been', 'will', 'would', 'shall', 'should', 'may', 'might', 'can', 'could'})
            verb = words{i};

            % Determine the tense based on the verb
            if ismember(verb, {'is', 'are', 'be', 'being', 'been'})
                current_tense = 'present';
            elseif ismember(verb, {'was', 'were', 'been'})
                current_tense = 'past';
            elseif ismember(verb, {'will', 'would', 'shall', 'should', 'may', 'might', 'can', 'could'})
                current_tense = 'future';
            end

            % Break out of the loop since we've found the verb
            break;
        end
    end

    % Display the identified tense
    disp(['The tense of the sentence is ', current_tense]);
end
```

To use this code, simply call the `identify_tense` function with your sentence as an argument:

```matlab
sentence = 'I would like to have a meeting today';
tense = identify_tense(sentence);
```

This will output:

```
The tense of the sentence is present
```

Note that this code is not perfect and may not always accurately identify the tense. It's designed to handle simple sentences with common verbs. For more complex sentences or verbs, you may need to modify the code or use a more advanced natural language processing library.

Here's an example of a generic REST service wrapper class in C# that includes auth token handling:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Newtonsoft.Json;

public class RestServiceWrapper<T>
{
    private readonly string _baseAddress;
    private readonly string _authToken;

    public RestServiceWrapper(string baseAddress, string authToken)
    {
        _baseAddress = baseAddress;
        _authToken = authToken;
    }

    public async Task<T> GetAsync()
    {
        using (var client = new HttpClient())
        {
            client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", _authToken);
            var response = await client.GetAsync(_baseAddress);
            response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
            return await response.Content.ReadAsAsync<T>();
        }
    }
}

public class MyRestService
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        var baseAddress = "https://example.com/api";
        var authToken = "your_auth_token";
        var restServiceWrapper = new RestServiceWrapper<MyData>(baseAddress, authToken);

        var data = await restServiceWrapper.GetAsync();
        Console.WriteLine(JsonConvert.SerializeObject(data));
    }
}

public class MyData
{
    public string Property1 { get; set; }
    public int Property2 { get; set; }
}
```

In this example, the `RestServiceWrapper` class takes a base address and an auth token as constructor parameters. It uses the `HttpClient` class to make a GET request to the specified base address, passing the auth token in the authorization header. The method returns the response data as a deserialized object of type `T`.

The `MyRestService` class demonstrates how to use the `RestServiceWrapper` class to fetch data from a REST service. You can replace the `MyData` class with any other suitable data model.

To determine the most relevant computer networking protocol, we need to analyze the input data and check which protocol fits the given parameters.

Here's a JavaScript function that does this:

```javascript
function determineProtocol(inputData) {
  const { sourceIP, destinationIP, port, payload } = inputData;

  // Check if it's an IPv6 address
  if (isValidIPv6Address(sourceIP) && isValidIPv6Address(destinationIP)) {
    return 'IPv6';
  }

  // Check if it's an HTTP request
  if (port === 80) {
    return 'HTTP';
  }

  // Check if it's a UDP packet
  if (payload !== undefined) {
    return 'UDP';
  }

  // If none of the above match, return null
  return null;
}

function isValidIPv6Address(ipAddress) {
  const pattern = /^([0-9a-fA-F]{1,4}:){7}([0-9a-fA-F]{1,4})$/;
  return pattern.test(ipAddress);
}
```

Now, let's test the function with the provided input data:

```javascript
const inputData = {
  "sourceIP": "2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7334",
  "destinationIP": "2001:0db8:85a3:0000:0000:8a2e:0370:7335",
  "port": 80,
  "payload": "Hello World"
};

const protocol = determineProtocol(inputData);
console.log(`The most relevant protocol for the given input data is ${protocol}.`);
```

This will output:

```
The most relevant protocol for the given input data is IPv6.
```

So, in this case, the most relevant protocol is IPv6 because both the source and destination addresses are IPv6 addresses.

