In the Model-View-Controller (MVC) pattern, the model represents the data and business logic of an application, while the view displays the data to the user. The controller acts as an intermediary between the model and the view, managing user input and updating the model and view accordingly.

When you add attributes to a model in MVC, it typically involves the following steps:

1. Define the attribute in the model class: In your model class, define a new property or field that represents the attribute you want to add. For example:
```csharp
public class MyModel
{
    public string Attribute1 { get; set; }
    public int Attribute2 { get; set; }
}
```
1. Update the view to display the new attribute: In your view, create a new element or control that will display the value of the new attribute. For example, if you're using ASP.NET MVC, you might add a new `<input>` field to your view:
```html
@model MyModel

<label for="attribute1">Attribute 1:</label>
<input type="text" id="attribute1" name="attribute1" value="@Model.Attribute1" />

<label for="attribute2">Attribute 2:</label>
<input type="number" id="attribute2" name="attribute2" value="@Model.Attribute2" />
```
1. Update the controller to handle the new attribute: In your controller, update any relevant methods to handle the new attribute. For example, if you're using ASP.NET MVC, you might add a new method to handle a form submission that includes the new attribute:
```csharp
public ActionResult Submit(MyModel model)
{
    // Handle the new attribute here
    // ...
}
```
2. Test your changes: Test your application to ensure that the new attribute is displayed correctly and that the controller is handling it properly.

Overall, adding attributes to a model in MVC involves defining the new attribute in the model class, updating the view to display the new attribute, updating the controller to handle the new attribute, and testing your changes. This process allows you to easily add new features and functionality to your application without having to modify the existing codebase.

Here's a Go program that parses a struct in a Go source file and generates an Avro schema from it. The program takes the name of the Go source file and the name of the struct as input, and outputs a file named `struct.avsc`.

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"io/ioutil"
	"regexp"
	"strings"

	"github.com/apache/avro/v2/schema"
	"github.com/apache/avro/v2/specific"
)

func main() {
	file := "example.go"
	structName := "MyStruct"

	content, err := ioutil.ReadFile(file)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Error reading file:", err)
		return
	}

	re := regexp.MustCompile(`type (\w+) struct {`)
	matches := re.FindStringSubmatch(string(content))
	if len(matches) != 2 {
		fmt.Println("Error parsing file:", "no match found for struct definition")
		return
	}

	structType := matches[1]

	schemaDef, err := parseStruct(structType)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Error parsing struct:", err)
		return
	}

	err = saveSchema(schemaDef, fmt.Sprintf("%s.avsc", structName))
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println("Error saving schema:", err)
		return
	}
}

func parseStruct(structType string) (*schema.Schema, error) {
	parts := strings.Split(structType, ".")
	if len(parts) != 2 {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("invalid struct type: %s", structType)
	}

	pkgPath := parts[0]
	structName := parts[1]

	// Import the package
	pkg, err := specific.ImportPackage(pkgPath)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("error importing package: %s", err)
	}

