Here's a simple C code snippet that converts `argv` to a C string:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *cstr = (char *)malloc(argc * sizeof(char *));

    for (int i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        strcat(cstr, argv[i]);
        if (i != argc - 1) {
            strcat(cstr, " ");
        }
    }

    printf("C string: %s\n", cstr);

    free(cstr);

    return 0;
}
```

This code creates a new character array `cstr` with enough space to store all the arguments in the `argv` array. It then iterates through the `argv` array and concatenates each argument to the `cstr` using the `strcat()` function. Finally, it prints the resulting C string and frees the memory allocated for the `cstr`.

